/* The header for this test is at the BOTTOM of this file, below the script.
   It is there because a constant inside a leading comment can swallow the
   rest of the file in the engine - see RMSTEST_56 and the directory README.
   Nothing below the script can do any harm. */

#const COUNT_A 7

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
  base_terrain GRASS

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>


<TERRAIN_GENERATION>


<OBJECTS_GENERATION>

  /* FLOOR - unconditional, before anything. Sanity that the file parsed and
     ran at all. */
  create_object FORAGE
  {
    number_of_objects 25
    set_gaia_object_only
  }

  /* ARM A, BASELINE - confirms #const COUNT_A 7 resolves normally in a
     numeric slot before #define ever touches the name. */
  create_object OLIVE_TREE
  {
    number_of_objects COUNT_A
    set_gaia_object_only
  }

  /* ARM A - #define layered on a name that ALREADY has a #const value,
     inside a start_random, exactly Capricious.rms's shape. */
  start_random
    percent_chance 100
      #define COUNT_A
    percent_chance 0
  end_random

  /* ARM A, TEST - what does COUNT_A resolve to now? */
  create_object GOLD
  {
    number_of_objects COUNT_A
    set_gaia_object_only
  }

  /* ARM B - #define on a name with NO prior #const at all - the bare case,
     with no fallback value to fall through to even if one exists. */
  start_random
    percent_chance 100
      #define COUNT_B
    percent_chance 0
  end_random

  /* ARM B, TEST - what does COUNT_B resolve to with nothing else defining
     it? */
  create_object STONE
  {
    number_of_objects COUNT_B
    set_gaia_object_only
  }

<CONNECTION_GENERATION>


/*
================================================================================
RMSTEST_69 - #define in a numeric slot: what value, if any, does the engine
actually read
docs/known-issues.md BUG-021, src/parser/parser.ts (isDefinedSymbol,
parser.ts:1086), src/preview/generator/instantiate.ts (constant resolution),
de-official-map-issues.md Sec.5 (Capricious.rms:880-883,
includes/herdable.inc)
================================================================================

THE QUESTION
Capricious.rms rolls HERDABLE_COUNT with #define, not #const, inside a
start_random; herdable.inc later reads it with `number_of_objects
HERDABLE_COUNT`. #define assigns no value in the engine (confirmed elsewhere
in this project - it is a presence flag, the mechanism guide-documented
conditionals like #ifdef check), so the roll almost certainly does nothing
useful - but "does nothing" is not the same claim as "resolves to a specific
number", and instantiate.ts's behaviour here has never been checked against
either reading. Our parser is already deliberately permissive:
isDefinedSymbol() treats a #define'd name and a #const'd name as
interchangeably "resolved" for diagnostic purposes, and a #define symbol
carries no valueToken, so no false warning fires and no fabricated value is
produced - correct under either candidate engine reading, which is exactly
why this needs a run rather than a code change.

TWO ARMS, BRACKETING BOTH SHAPES BUG-021 NAMES
Arm A matches Capricious's actual shape: a name that already has a #const
default (herdable.inc's own), then a #define layered on top inside a
start_random. Arm B is the bare case - #define on a name with NO #const
anywhere - which brackets the other side: if #define and #const genuinely
share one namespace and first-definition-wins the way #const-vs-#const
already does in this project's own measured model, arm A's #define should be
inert (the earlier #const wins) but arm B has no earlier definition to fall
back to at all, so the two arms can disagree even under one single
consistent model of how #define behaves.

Both #defines sit inside a start_random pinned 100/0, exactly Capricious's
own shape, in case the engine's numeric-slot answer for #define depends on
being inside a conditional block rather than at top level - untested either
way until this runs.

READ-OFF TABLE
  FORAGE (floor)              expect ~25    file parsed and ran; if 0, stop
                                             and check for an unrelated
                                             script error first
  OLIVE_TREE (arm A baseline) expect ~7     confirms #const COUNT_A resolves
                                             normally BEFORE #define touches
                                             it - if this is not ~7, the
                                             harness itself is broken and
                                             neither test arm is readable
  GOLD (arm A test)           expect ~7     COUNT_A still resolves to its
                                             #const value after the #define -
                                             first-definition-wins, #define
                                             is a numeric no-op when a #const
                                             already exists for the name
                               expect 0 (or command silently places nothing)
                                             the #define OVERWRITES the slot
                                             with something the numeric
                                             evaluator reads as zero/
                                             unresolved, even though a #const
                                             default already existed
                               expect ~25 (falls through to
                               number_of_objects's own guide default instead)
                                             a third reading - the slot is
                                             genuinely undefined and the
                                             engine substitutes its own
                                             argument default rather than 0
  STONE (arm B test, no
         #const fallback exists at all)
                               expect 0 (or nothing placed)  matches "genuinely
                                             unresolved, no value at all" -
                                             the CLEANEST reading if it also
                                             matches GOLD's result
                               expect ~25 (number_of_objects's own default)
                                             same third reading as above, now
                                             confirmed with no fallback #const
                                             in the picture to complicate it

READING THE COMBINATION
  GOLD~7, STONE reads 0/default   #define and #const share a namespace and
    first-definition-wins exactly as this project's #const-vs-#const model
    already assumes (Sec.2.1's shared-id finding, one level up) - only when a
    prior #const exists does the name resolve to anything. instantiate.ts
    should resolve #define-only names the same way STONE reads, and #define-
    over-#const names the same way GOLD reads (both should already happen for
    free if the resolver reads the LATEST value bound to the name and #define
    never binds one - confirm this is what instantiate.ts actually does
    before closing the bug, per this project's own "check the code, don't
    reason about it" rule).
  GOLD and STONE read the SAME as each other, whatever that value is   simpler
    model: #define's presence has no bearing on the pre-existing #const at
    all, in either direction. The prior #const value survives untouched
    where one exists, and where none exists the result is whatever the
    engine does for a fully unresolved numeric symbol - confirm which by
    checking GOLD equals the OLIVE_TREE baseline exactly.
  Either the script fails to parse in-game, or the map comes back visibly
  broken/blank downstream of the first start_random   record this
    separately - it would mean an in-block #define does something more
    disruptive than a numeric-slot question, closer in shape to BUG-012's
    comment hazard than to an ordinary undefined-symbol case.

GENERATE AT NORMAL (200), ANY PLAYER COUNT. ONE RUN - four fixed values, not
a statistical question.

READ WITH
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>"
  The default object histogram is the whole reading - four names against
  four expected counts.
================================================================================
*/
